WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.660
Cut a nerve in your arm, and its axons can grow back.

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.760
Injure your spinal cord, and they mostly do not.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.240
That is why a spinal cord injury usually leaves a lasting loss.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.060
Two things hold central axons back.

5
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.760
The tissue around them blocks regrowth.

6
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.840
And adult central neurons turn their own growth program down.

7
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.900
In a nerve of the arm, the neuron is ready to grow, and the cells around it help.

8
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:29.900
www .mooji .org

9
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.280
How do we know both matter?

10
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.500
In rats, a peripheral nerve graft, set as a bridge from the medulla to the spinal

11
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:42.160
cord, let central axons grow about thirty millimetres, until they met central tissue

12
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:48.580
again. In mice, deleting a gene called PTEN from neurons of the eye let many axons

13
00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:50.620
regrow through a crushed optic nerve.

14
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:53.643
A cut splits each axon in two.

15
00:00:54.483 --> 00:00:57.043
The part joined to the cell body can grow a new tip.

16
00:00:57.883 --> 00:01:02.363
The part beyond the cut falls to pieces after about a day and a half, by a program

17
00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:08.063
of its own. In mice that lack a gene called Sarm1, cut axons stay whole for weeks.

18
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:15.267
An enzyme called NMNAT2 is short-lived, and the cell body must keep sending it down

19
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:17.987
the axon. A cut stops the supply.

20
00:01:18.807 --> 00:01:23.707
Its raw material, NMN, builds up and, in the leading model, switches SARM1 on.

21
00:01:24.627 --> 00:01:26.207
SARM1 cleaves NAD+.

22
00:01:26.207 --> 00:01:31.047
Energy fails, calcium rises, and calpains cut the axon apart.

23
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:36.870
In a nerve of the arm, Schwann cells, the glia of peripheral nerve, turn into repair

24
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:42.730
cells. They help clear the old myelin, call in macrophages from the blood, and line

25
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:47.710
up into tubes that guide the regrowing axon back toward its target, often at around

26
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.710
a millimetre a day.

27
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:52.073
In the spinal cord, debris clears slowly.

28
00:01:53.013 --> 00:01:57.893
Proteins in central myelin, such as Nogo, collapse growth cones, though removing

29
00:01:57.893 --> 00:01:58.893
them alone does little.

30
00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:02.453
The lesion also fills with growth-blocking molecules.

31
00:02:03.593 --> 00:02:07.573
Astrocytes form a border around it, but that border also limits the spread of

32
00:02:07.573 --> 00:02:11.493
damage. So the aim is to change the scar, not remove it.

33
00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:13.747
So there are two problems.

34
00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:19.947
Blocking SARM1 can keep axons from breaking down, but a fully severed stump cannot

35
00:02:19.947 --> 00:02:21.187
rejoin its cell body.

36
00:02:22.387 --> 00:02:26.427
Function returns only if the attached part regrows and connects with the right

37
00:02:26.427 --> 00:02:30.167
target. In rodents, each fix helps, usually partly.

38
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:33.507
None yet restores function reliably in people.
